# Lab 8 — Node.js and MySQL with Docker Compose

This guide documents the Lab 8 setup. The final files belong in your `ivolve-nti` repository. The `iVolveApp` repository in the assignment is the source for the required application.

## 1. Create the Lab 8 directory in your repository

```bash
cd ~/ivolve-nti
mkdir -p "IVOLVE LABS/Docker/Lab8"
cd "IVOLVE LABS/Docker/Lab8"
```

## 2. Download the reference application and copy its files into Lab 8

The temporary directory is only used to obtain the application files. Keep your repository's `origin` unchanged; the final files remain under `~/ivolve-nti/IVOLVE LABS/Docker/Lab8`.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/iVolveApp.git /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference
cp -a /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference/frontend .
cp /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference/Dockerfile \
   /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference/db.js \
   /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference/package.json \
   /tmp/ivolveapp-lab8-reference/server.js .
```

Expected files include `frontend/index.html`, `frontend/assets/ivolve-logo.png`, `Dockerfile`, `db.js`, `package.json`, and `server.js`.

## 3. Create the Docker Compose configuration

Create or update `docker-compose.yml` inside the Lab 8 directory:

```yaml
services:
  app:
    build: .
    image: lab8-app:latest
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"
    environment:
      DB_HOST: db
      DB_USER: ivolve_user
      DB_PASSWORD: lab8password
      DB_NAME: ivolve
    volumes:
      - app_logs:/app/logs
    depends_on:
      db:
        condition: service_healthy
    networks:
      - ivolve-net

  db:
    image: mysql:8.0
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: rootLab8password
      MYSQL_DATABASE: ivolve
      MYSQL_USER: ivolve_user
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: lab8password
    volumes:
      - db_data:/var/lib/mysql
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "mysqladmin ping -h localhost -uroot -p$${MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD}"]
      interval: 5s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 10
    networks:
      - ivolve-net

volumes:
  app_logs:
  db_data:

networks:
  ivolve-net:
    driver: bridge
```

## 4. Validate the configuration and start the application

Run these commands from the Lab 8 directory:

```bash
docker compose config
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose ps
docker compose logs --tail=40 app
```

Confirm that `db` is `healthy`, `app` is `Up`, and host port 3000 is mapped to container port 3000.

## 5. Verify the application and database connection

```bash
curl http://localhost:3000/health
curl http://localhost:3000/ready
```

Open `http://localhost:3000` in a browser inside the VM to view the application. Successful health and readiness responses confirm that the application is running and connected to the database.

## 6. Push the Lab 8 files to your GitHub repository

```bash
cd ~/ivolve-nti
git status --short
git add -- "IVOLVE LABS/Docker/Lab8"
git commit -m "Update Docker Lab 8 to iVolveApp"
git pull --rebase origin main
git push origin main
```

The `git add` command stages only the Lab 8 directory, leaving unrelated files such as `Lab7/backend-data` unstaged.

## 7. Push the application image to Docker Hub

Replace `DOCKERHUB_USERNAME` with your Docker Hub username. Create the `ivolve-app` repository on Docker Hub first if it does not already exist.

```bash
docker login -u DOCKERHUB_USERNAME
docker tag lab8-app:latest DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/ivolve-app:lab8
docker push DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/ivolve-app:lab8
```

Enter your password or Personal Access Token when `docker login` prompts in the terminal.

## 8. Stop the services after finishing

```bash
docker compose down
```

This removes the containers and network while retaining the MySQL volume. Do not use `docker compose down -v` unless you also intend to delete the database data.

